---
import TownPage from '../components/TownPage.astro';
import { study } from '../data/studies/northern-westchester-2026';
---

<TownPage slug="web-design-peekskill">
  <section class="regional" aria-labelledby="regional-h">
    <h2 id="regional-h">The regional picture, by type of business</h2>
    <p>
      The full study covered {study.sitesFound} independent businesses across Peekskill and its neighbors.
      Here is how each type of business did.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Type of business</th><th scope="col">Sites scored</th><th scope="col">Typical score</th><th scope="col">Typical time to main content</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {study.byCategory.map((c) => (
          <tr><td>{c.name}</td><td>{c.scored}</td><td>{Math.round(c.median)}</td><td>{c.medianLcp.toFixed(1)} s</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>What this means if you run a business here</h3>
    <ul class="points">
      <li><strong>Restaurants feel it most.</strong> People look up a menu or hours on their phone, often on the way. Restaurant sites in the region took a typical {study.byCategory[0].medianLcp.toFixed(1)} seconds to show their main content.</li>
      <li><strong>Google notices speed.</strong> Page experience on phones is part of how Google evaluates sites, so a slow site can lose ground to a faster competitor down the street.</li>
      <li><strong>Broken links cost customers quietly.</strong> {study.noResponse + study.pageNotFound} of the {study.sitesFound} website links listed for local businesses on the map did not work.</li>
      <li><strong>Layout jumps are common.</strong> On {study.layoutShiftPct}% of sites, the page shifted noticeably while loading, which leads to mis-taps on phones.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="method" aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <h2 id="method-h">How the study was done</h2>
    <p>
      I collected every business in {study.area} that lists a website on OpenStreetMap, the public map database,
      and left out national chains, regional chains, and government pages. That left {study.sitesFound} independent
      local businesses and organizations. I tested each homepage once with Google PageSpeed Insights using its mobile
      setting, which simulates a mid-range phone on a slow 4G connection. Visitors on fast Wi-Fi will see quicker
      loads, but every site was measured the same way. Each business was assigned to a town by its map location.
      Results are reported as totals only, and no business is named.
    </p>
  </section>
</TownPage>

<style>
  section { margin-top: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem); }
  h2 { font-size: var(--step-2); text-wrap: balance; max-width: 30ch; }
  h3 { font-size: var(--step-1); margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; }
  .regional > p, .method p { color: #c4ccda; max-width: 64ch; margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .method p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; max-width: 48rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.7rem 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 500; font-size: var(--step--1); }
  td:not(:first-child), th:not(:first-child) { text-align: right; }
  .points { max-width: 64ch; padding-left: 1.1rem; color: #c4ccda; }
  .points li + li { margin-top: 0.9rem; }
  .points li::marker { color: var(--amber); }
  .points strong { color: var(--text); }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) { th, td { padding: 0.55rem 0.4rem; font-size: 0.85rem; } }
</style>
